wifi_meshmgr: guard unhandled mesh exception numbers

nss_wifi_meshmgr_config_mesh_exception() assigns ifnum only for the
three exception numbers its switch handles, then passes it to
nss_wifi_mesh_msg_init() as the destination interface of a firmware
config message. The switch has no default, so any exception number the
switch does not list -- NSS_WIFI_MESH_DS_MESH_PATH_EXPIRY (4) included,
a documented value -- is sent to an uninitialized interface number.

The only in-tree caller is the ath11k exc_rate_limit debugfs write,
which parses "%u %u %u" with no range check, so a value of 4 or anything
else reaches this switch. What the firmware then does with the message
depends on whatever the uninitialised slot happens to hold: the two
interface numbers the function does validate are checked against their
dynamic-interface types a few lines above, and this one is checked
against nothing.

No vendor source handles case 4 either: pristine 11.4 and 12.5
qca-nss-clients and qosmio's shipped 11.4 tree all end the switch at
NSS_WIFI_MESH_US_MESH_PATH_NOT_FOUND with no default. Reject the value
instead of inventing a mapping -- the DS (Eth - Wi-Fi) direction matches
the encap interface, but no source states it, so dispatching it would be
a guess. This leaves the DS mesh path expiry rate-limit configuration
unsupported.

Add a default that warns, drops the reference taken at the top of the
function, and returns NSS_WIFI_MESHMGR_FAILURE_INVALID_EXCEPTION_NUM.
That constant already exists for exactly this case.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/wifi_meshmgr/nss_wifi_meshmgr.c
+++ b/wifi_meshmgr/nss_wifi_meshmgr.c
@@ -1640,6 +1640,11 @@
 	case NSS_WIFI_MESH_US_MESH_PATH_NOT_FOUND:
 		ifnum = decap_ifnum;
 		break;
+
+	default:
+		nss_wifi_meshmgr_warn("%px: invalid exception num: %u\n", &wmgr_ctx, wmrlc->exception_num);
+		nss_wifi_meshmgr_ref_dec(wmesh_ctx);
+		return NSS_WIFI_MESHMGR_FAILURE_INVALID_EXCEPTION_NUM;
 	}

